[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2,router3
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: L3 interconnect addressing between router2 and router3 (link_r2_r3), not router0’s RIP or egress.

Mechanism: Location A hosts (172.16.10.0/24) forward to router0 (172.16.10.1). Router0’s RIP is running and its WAN egress is up (eth2 10.16.1.1/24), with routes to remote nets via 10.16.1.2. However, traffic toward Location C must traverse router1 → router2 → router3. Both router2 and router3 have eth4 configured with the same IP 192.168.100.2/24. Because router2’s next-hop to 172.18.50/24 and 172.18.51/24 is 192.168.100.2 on eth4, this duplicate causes ARP/neighbor resolution to self, so packets are never forwarded across the r2–r3 link, yielding timeouts from Location A to Location C.

Localization: 
- Router0: RIP healthy and egress fine (show ip rip shows routes; can ping 10.16.1.2; routes installed).
- Router1: can reach router2 (10.20.1.2) but cannot reach 172.18.51.0/24.
- Router2: eth4 UP 192.168.100.2/24; routes to 172.18.50/51 via 192.168.100.2.
- Router3: eth4 also UP 192.168.100.2/24. Evidence: “ip -brief addr” on both routers shows the duplicate.

Root cause: Duplicate IP address 192.168.100.2/24 configured on both router2 eth4 and router3 eth4 on the r2–r3 interconnect.

Corrective fix: Assign unique IPs on the r2–r3 link (e.g., router2 eth4 192.168.100.1/24, router3 eth4 192.168.100.2/24), then clear ARP/neighbor entries (ip neigh flush dev eth4). Verify mutual ping across 192.168.100.0/24 and end-to-end reachability from PC4/PC5 to 172.18.51.0/24. No action needed on router0’s RIP or egress. 
=== END ===